//! Pinned harmonypy reference values for LISI (§7.19, ORG-7.21-4).
//!
//! GENERATED FILE — do not edit by hand. See `# Regenerating` below.
//!
//! # Why an external reference at all
//!
//! SCX's LISI asked for `3 * perplexity` neighbours where harmonypy's
//! effective neighbourhood is `3 * perplexity - 1`: it retrieves
//! `3 * perplexity` from `NearestNeighbors` and drops column 0, the
//! self-match, while SCX's sweep skips `j == i` as it collects. The
//! derivation was written out three times — `LisiConfig::default`,
//! `pyscx/src/accel/lisi.rs` and `rscx/src/lisi.rs` — and all three said
//! `3 * perplexity`. The one harmonypy comparison in the tree
//! (`test_harmony_validation.py::test_lisi_matches_harmonypy_per_cell`)
//! ran at `atol = 1e-2` and passed with the bug.
//!
//! # Provenance
//!
//! | constant | produced by |
//! |---|---|
//! | `LISI_X`, `LISI_LABELS` | this generator's RNG — the fixture INPUT |
//! | [`LISI_EXPECTED`] | harmonypy `harmonypy.lisi.compute_lisi` |
//! | [`LISI_OFF_BY_ONE`] | the same, with only the retrieval width patched |
//!
//! harmonypy 0.2.0
//! numpy 2.4.4
//! scikit-learn 1.8.0
//! pandas 2.3.3
//!
//! # Why the perplexity is 4 and not the default 30
//!
//! At perplexity 30 the neighbourhood is 89 cells, so on any fixture
//! small enough to read as literals one neighbour more or less changes
//! almost nothing and the arm would pass against either convention. At
//! perplexity 4 the neighbourhood is 11 of 60 cells and one extra
//! neighbour moves LISI by up to 2.435e-02 — checked by the generator, which
//! refuses a fixture that cannot separate the two conventions.
//!
//! # Regenerating
//!
//! ```text
//! .venv/bin/python benchmarks/scripts/generate_harmony_references.py lisi \
//!     > scx-accel/src/lisi_reference_values.rs
//! ```

